if you haven't already please silence your cellphone's all right good evening mayor and city council my name is Eric Trowbridge but most people know me plainly by my initials et the headmaster the executive director of the array school locate on 1607 Capitol Avenue in downtown Cheyenne and I am speaking on behalf of array Inc array is Wyoming's first accelerated technology and design school taking students through a seven-month workforce training program that teaches web and mobile app development graphic design computer science fundamentals from Stanford University and about three dozen other different technologies and disciplines the reason I am providing this back story is that it should be fairly clear how important shines internet infrastructure is to our school and

as potential future growth here in southeast Wyoming we're proud to call Cheyenne home and also proud of our accomplishments we've graduated three classes that's 2016 with almost all of them being placed into our growing technology sector here in the state most of them right here in Cheyenne the average salary of array is about forty six thousand five hundred and our students have used their new careers to purchase homes start companies and contribute back to our city over the last two years we have attracted to tech companies either to Wyoming or to downtown Cheyenne the first is helix technologies who are based out of San Francisco and opened their administrative office here in downtown Cheyenne last year in the paramount building over the next few years it may even begin to transition some of their software developers from the Bay Area to downtown Cheyenne the other is in lumen technologies which hired one of our graduates from our second class to open their Wyoming office and

lumen is based in Reno Nevada with offices in San Francisco Seattle and Austin Texas so you can understand why then the mayor called on me in January to chair her broadband task force as part of her State of the City address I was excited I was excited to begin work on this incredibly important discussion about moving our community forward and preparing it to grow with the necessary infrastructure I was excited to work with a small team appointed by the mayor to begin research on how we could build and maintain a competitive broadband market that would benefit every resident business and school in Cheyenne our director from the mayor was to explore all options and come up with a plan that outlined what we could do to make this happen we looked at everything from the municipality broadband options private public partnerships and

the private industry solutions we try to find other communities with some or makeups of Cheyenne that we could use the study these different approaches could they work here how would they work here what would these where were these cities doing and what was the impact to their communities we looked at Amman Idaho Salt Lake City we partnered with the city of Lincoln Nebraska and a handful of other municipalities and as you know it wasn't hard to find them clearly many many cities big and small are all looking for their own answers too so instead of spending too much time or money or reinventing the wheel or digging super deep into data points we try to identify common initiatives that these cities were doing to prepare for future technologies like 5g fiber-to-the-home and

business and different smart city initiatives and that is exactly what this resolution contains Sickler six recommendations that we felt were in common with cities who had are or will be moving forward with similar projects the challenge of building and thriving technology sector is that the infrastructure must be there before it can begin to take hold Internet infrastructure is a different animal it's very difficult to recruit new tech companies to Cheyenne with a promise so for example you know a Cheyenne leads economic development agency wouldn't use we have internet in Cheyenne as a selling point to bring a company here we need to make the investments both time and money to 1st retain who we have a true we have and

attract new companies here that will add to our local economy and provide high-paying jobs who our local labor market is infrastructure expensive sure it is fiber isn't cheap and we all know that digging up the ground and streets isn't cheap either building conduits and small cell towers isn't cheap but you know what if there is probably one thing that Cheyenne could invest in that is the safest bet that we could possibly do it isn't investing in our Internet infrastructure it is simply one of the safest investments our city could make for its future the demand for faster internet speeds cellular coverage etc isn't going away and will only continue to grow with each passing day we cannot predict the future no one knows what they or their business will do with the in the next few years information technology and

our dependence on it continues to grow exponentially so we can't predict the future but we can do our best to prepare for it it will be time and money well spent our ultimate goal with this resolution was to lay the foundation for further exploration and consideration the resolution was designed to create an atmosphere that encourages competitive broadband services in our city allowing smaller and medium-sized Internet service providers to come and do business here as we all know competition is great for everyone our goal should be to lower the cost of deployment for private companies plain and simple if we can do this consumers businesses and schools will all benefit from more options pricing and speeds based on their current and

future needs a competitive broadband market is a major decision factor for technology companies looking to relocate or grow in a community our current offerings are simply just not enough Shayan does not have many choices for their Internet service provider speeds and pricing are mostly lacking reliability needs improvements just last week we started a brand new class at array and it's actually our first female majority class by the way our internet @array has dropped just about 5 different times since introducing a new 4k live-streaming telogen that we're trying to pioneer for Wyoming next year when we watch videos I have to ask my students to turn off their Wi-Fi so we can watch the video without that dreaded buffering loader we've upgraded to the fastest package possible within our budget I'm excited about charters announcement last month and

I'm really pleased by their investments and I think it is a great and wonderful addition to improving our experience here and I do definitely sincerely want to thank them for their support and investments but we should never stop that doesn't solve everything we must keep going and keep pushing and continue to always look for ways that we can stay ahead of the curve we are going to stay ahead of that curve when you consider the following six recommendations one Shayan should consider a dedicated person to the Internet infrastructure initiatives in the near future Shyam's needs will continue to differ from most of the communities across the state and should definitely partner with the new broadband manager at the Wyoming Business Council too we should consider looking into building a publicly owned open access conduit network Lincoln Nebraska has been doing this for the last six years and

saw the cost per megabits go from $50 for one megabit per second and in the last six years that has dropped to 32 cents over the course of six years of building the public conduit system three adopt a one dig policy this just makes sense when the ground is up private entities should be informed to have and have access to the right-of-way and build new conduits or maintain them every time we dig up a street to access the right-of-way we create all the issues that were all too familiar with unfortunately potholes road closures broken sidewalks etc a one dig policy will help keep our streets and sidewalks in the best shape that they can and

provide better services to our right-of-ways than when the ground is up number four adopt a wireless facility ordinance in preparation for the pending 5g networks the city of Cheyenne must change his code to allow for the quick development and deployment of small cells throughout the city without this crucial infrastructure piece Cheyenne simply will not have 5g networks when they launch in the coming years plain and simple five eliminate or reduce the high right-of-way access fees if we go back to our mission which was to lower the cost of deployment for private companies then this is one of the major barriers of entry to create competitive broadband market and lastly number six build a downtown public Wi-Fi system this recommendation actually came to us from a few different individuals and

companies who have been wanting to see a free public Wi-Fi system installed in our downtown after some interesting conversations and connections we were able to find some private companies who were interested in helping contribute funds for the initial infrastructure build-out public Wi-Fi systems have well have become welcomed additions to many towns and cities across the country they are just waiting for the city to approve this resolution so that an RFP can be sent out for bid wouldn't that be great for all of us so in summary this resolution lays a foundation from moving forward and continuing the conversations and strategies beyond this chamber it will significantly help set the groundwork for a competitive broadband market lowering the cost of deployment for private companies setting the stage for future technology innovations that are looming and

making sure as a city that we are prepared for them and most importantly this resolution sends a strong message to the rest of the state and region that we want to make our capital city everything that it has the potential to become and I want to sincerely thank the mayor and my broadband task force for all the hard work that they put into this resolution over the last eight months when the task force was created in January I gave it an internal nickname called the Northstar initiative last year I saw a beautiful satellite photo of the Front Range from space and it was easy to pick out where Denver and for Collins and Boulder were and

then there was this little black gap right above Wellington to Cheyenne and then you could see the twinkling lights of Cheyenne the article referred to Cheyenne as a northernmost terminus of the developing Front Range and it was kind of this beautiful image which made Cheyenne look like the North Star and I just love that analogy and there's another beautiful analogy that Cheyenne should become the North Star of the state of Wyoming leading the way by example on how we're going to plan on growing diversifying our economies and showing what can be done when we work together and are committed to the future of this great city we all call home Cheyenne thank you and I'm open for questions thank

you and just for out there future speakers I think we're going to do is just ask Council if they have any questions of every speaker as they come up rather than having people go back and forth so are there any questions of Erick Trowbridge ET questions all right thank you very much are there public discussion Ben Rowland former member of the Cheyenne broadband task force I'm gonna make my comments very very brief because et covered a lot of ground and I think everybody here is probably topic before either in personal conversation or during the committee session last week one of the primary points that I wanted to make was basically that the recommendations and the resolution tonight they are in my mind viewed as sort of endpoint recommendations in some ways and

they don't necessarily spell out all the steps that might need to be taken to get there and I just want to make that point because I know I've heard a lot of discussion about the data that might be necessary to get to that point and because reference to that data is not included in the body the memo I don't want anybody to think that it wouldn't be a part of the development of the project or the ordinance or whichever recommendation we're referring to so I just wanted to provide a little bit of feedback on that I also want to make another point that generally for all of the recommendations whether or not we're talking about a policy or the implementation of a new ordinance or even the building of any of the projects referenced in the memo almost all

those would require something to come back to the City Council so you the governing body would continue to basically retain control over a lot of these projects or recommendations moving forward so we don't want anybody to be under the impression that a vote on the resolution tonight is a vote to pull out your shovels and break ground tomorrow and I don't say that to minimize the importance of you know or doing now but just to remind members of the governing body that you will continually have control over any of these policies or ordinances or projects moving forward so I'm going to keep my comments to that and of course if you do have any questions I'll be here thanks

thank you welcome back to the podium mr. Rowland any questions of mr. Rowland thank you any other public comment good evening hi honorable mayor esteemed City Council members my name is Abbie Petersen and I'm here on behalf of just as a citizen and I live at two five one seven vanillin and Avenue here in Cheyenne Wyoming and I work from home I am an online educator for Wyoming Connections Academy and I just thought I would come and tell you a little bit about my experience and the experience of my students my students are all over the state of Wyoming and we are online all day long and my experience of the Internet is that it's slow and

