predominantly online which has an increasing sector every year and also for the education of our students thanks thank you thank you for being here tonight appreciate good evening good evening my name is Austin Hine and I'm a public policy analyst for a Wyoming Liberty group I am new to the Cheyenne area as of June of this year previously I reside in Washington DC employed at the United States House of Representatives and prior to that I lived in Fort Collins Colorado so pretty familiar with the area I attended Colorado State University and studied political science and economics and during that time I also worked in the state legislature in Colorado I moved to Cheyenne not only because of new employment opportunities but also because I believe that Wyoming is a bastion of freedom and

free enterprise however I fear that this publicly operated broadband resolution threatens the very tenants of that freedom let me be clear I do not in doubt the intentions of this council nor of the members of the assembled exploratory task force this council should absolutely consider opportunities to expand and improve internet access for Cheyenne's population however I believe that more harm than good will result from the passage of this resolution and I believe that for three main reasons I have three main concerns what is the financial viability of the proposal what with this proposal lead to better internet services for Cheyenne and is this a proper role government starting my first concern I ask that the appointed task force has fully considered the cost and

time investment in executing this resolution the city of Cheyenne would be responsible for taking trenches and installing thousands of yards of cable throughout the city of Cheyenne Cheyenne is over 20 years behind on the creation of this infrastructure how long would it realistically take to catch up on the network infrastructure that private companies spent years creating Charter is on the verge of introducing it gigabit internet speeds in the coming months and it may take years for the city of Cheyenne to reach the same speeds and availability innovation in the isp sector is exponentially growing gigabit speeds have only recently become viable in general and gigabit speed standards will not last long any locality that considers providing Internet service must be willing to dedicate time money personnel and

into keeping pace with the ever-expanding Internet infrastructure there are countless other strains that this resolution would put on the public for example server farms consume an enormous amount of energy and would in turn consume an exorbitant amount of funds to properly operate adopting this resolution would commit millions of taxpayer dollars to developing and maintaining a whole new Internet infrastructure that would quickly become Cheyenne's most expensive city project there's a stark difference between Internet infrastructure and say sewer systems while sewer systems power lines require upkeep they do not often require innovation and overhaul demanded of the Internet service provider industry the private sector has demonstrated its willingness to undertake these challenges already so I asked what is the necessity for the public sector and

exists role because of this challenge I am concerned that Cheyenne's ability to keep pace growing quality and service we have seen the private ISP business my concern is that Cheyenne well will constantly be one or more steps behind the private sector as I mentioned private companies are rolling out gigabit speeds in Cheyenne as we speak well Cheyenne be able to develop and implement comparable speeds by the time that charter and CenturyLink and others introduce two gigabit speeds I fear the possibility that we will have public contractors installing fiber-optic cables all while the private sector has invented a new mode of delivering Internet service that Cheyenne will not be able to compete with whether it's by satellite or whatever else will Cheyenne be able to adapt and

keep pace with the competition given the national limitation the city government while public while private companies dedicate the majority of their time and money innovating and creating new ways to deliver service to their customers the city of Cheyenne may be unable to dedicate the same amount of resources to the same projects I am concerned about what message descends to Cheyenne's current ISPs I understand that 0.5 in the taskforce conclusion is meant to incentivize ISPs to remain in the market we cannot reasonably ask private companies to accept competition for the public sector especially new ISPs governments can run at profit losses governments don't have to be reactionary to their customer bases and governments do not have to sell their product simply residing in Cheyenne we'd be asking our residents to subsidize and

pay for a service that may not even want private companies do not have a luxury to operate under this model passing this resolution would disincentivize further investment by ISPs in our local economy even eliminating right-of-way access fees would not be enough to invite more competition into a market that contains a public auction option consumers would only be incentivized to utilize this new public utility because they would already be paying for it with their tax dollars economic rule of law would suggest that this resolution would cause ISPs to exit the market not enter we will not get more choices we will get less I think this makes the question as to whether more internet or providing internet is a proper role of government internet may be essential in the 21st century lifestyle that is not necessitate government intervention the vast majority of Cheyenne currently has two or more ISP options to choose from which is more than you could say for the rest of the state the private sector actively and

adequately provided internet service to the residents of Cheyenne so why his public internet necessary it was mentioned earlier that Lincoln Nebraska uses these public fees just did a quick google search and they're not even providing the fastest speeds in the city they're not even providing the cheapest is this council convinced that the city can provide a higher quality service at a cheap rate than the ISPs who have operated in shy and over 20 years would Cheyenne be forced to consider tax increases to keep up with the expanding cost associated with Internet service I can point to my hometown of Fort Collins who recently adopted similar visions and began the process of creating a public internet service so

far as project has proved to be a logistical bureaucratic nightmare that will drain the city of millions and millions of dollars and resources you do not have to look far past our backyard to see the public internet services are not easy or cheap to implement and maintain both Fort Collins and Longmont Colorado have had major struggles both these cities have larger tax base ylim Cheyenne des so how can we expect the citizens of Cheyenne to perform at higher levels or the city of Cheyenne to perform at higher levels Cheyenne would do well to learn from for Collins's mistakes this council should pause and consider generation-long consequences of creating public internet service nobel prize-winning economist Milton Friedman what said there is nothing more permanent than a temporary government program so

I asked this council if they're willing to commit a long term future of the city to a new public utility a system that would outlive my children my children's children to be economically viable instead I recommend that this council consider free market solutions to internet access to invite more competition better service and lower costs to the public Cheyenne City Council should consider eliminating or lowering public right away access fees and franchise fees for both future an existing ISPs this wouldn't actually invite the competition and provide more options and provide better service without the burden on taxpayers thank you for your time and hope for quite less of questions thank you are there questions any questions thank

you for your comments tonight they're appreciated thank you good evening hello my name is Adam Marsh and I live at 901 country-club Avenue I would like to speak first off that after 10 years in Wyoming having moved here from Idaho I like it and my one real complaint the one thing that I'd like to see more of is more growth in the community more effort to bring in new services and improve the services that we have and I think this is a great effort to do that and I'd like to say something specifically towards what the Wyoming Liberty group representative just said he brought up some good points some valid points some things that we should be thinking about and

potentially concerned about but all of the things that he brought up are for the next step on the road this resolution is to take one step further and begin looking at actual projects begin looking at what are the actual costs of putting the pipe in the ground who are the companies that would actually be willing to lay fiber through that pipe who would we really partner with and what would the actual costs be that's when we should start asking the questions of is it economically feasible for us is it something that we can sustain over the long term this resolution should allow us to get there we should be able to ask those questions and

actually dig into those numbers and to vote that down now before we actually know what the economic viability of this is I think would be detrimental to this community and I hope that people would be willing to take that next step and actually look at the numbers and the true feasibility of this project so I would encourage members of the council to vote affirmative on this thank you thank you are there any questions okay hearing none thank you we appreciate your comments my name is Dale Steinberg and I'm president of the greater Cheyenne Chamber of Commerce I guess I would start by saying I neither think that this is a challenge to the foundation of our democracy nor do I think this is a panacea that will solve all

problems it's an either one of those I do think we're talking about a topic that is very important to business in our community as somebody who represents over a thousand businesses here in Cheyenne it is an issue that pops up for a lot of different reasons so I commend the mayor and and in the taskforce that did their work and you can hear about great work that Eric's are doing at his school and and there's a lot of exciting things happening related to tech in Cheyenne but as I was sitting there thinking about this a couple of things popped into my mind one thing I thought about was that about 18 months ago the chamber picked this issue up and

we looked at costs all up and down the Front Range and a big assumption is being made that we are more expensive for the same service than all of those right but a lot of people like stuff for free so even though it might not be more expensive than those other communities than our competitors it's still still not good enough and I was thinking about this project sitting right out here in front and I wonder what the approach of the city would be if I bid on this project and I was the cheapest bidder and I showed up with a horse-drawn buggy and a shovel and said I'm gonna do the park right across the road he said well you wouldn't even give me the time of day and

I said okay well as the city why don't you buy me a brand-new shiny truck and buy me all the equipment that it would take to do it and I can still bring the bid in cheaper than the guy that I'm competing against and of course it would be ludicrous for the city to do that but in some ways that's kind of what we're talking about here so I I guess my point is there's a there's a lot of things with this that the chamber strongly supports we we know we want to keep growing we know we want to be prepared for 5g we know we want faster speeds and areas we know we have kids in poverty that need internet access to be able to have a good future we know that in historic buildings downtown there are places where it's very expensive to get that hooked up so

the majority of the items on here we would strongly support but how they are done is very important and always think that the people have paid the freight over the years the providers that have been in our community the providers that have literally put us on the map tell you when you go to Fort Collins or you go to Omaha and talk to the Chamber directors there which I do and they're there in awe of what we have in Cheyenne we have a giant river of Internet sometimes we have a problem hooking into that River and we need to fix that but there are we have a lot of good things here so I'm not here to tell you to kill this I said we support a lot of what's on here what I would tell you is is that we need private sector business at the table having a big piece of this discussion and

you know just just like it I always get a little nervous at the word free Wi-Fi downtown free to hoop right I'm all for that right so the Chamber has actually supplied free Wi-Fi for a long time and finally the Depot now supply there's free Wi-Fi all over the plaza there's a booster and in the plaza certainly it needs some upgrade but as Eric said earlier there's some there's some possibilities for that right but at the end of the day somebody pays for that somebody somewhere is paying for that you know he's talking to the chamber director from Chattanooga which was held up for a long time and as kind of the the example of how cities could own internet around the country and

